[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65832,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiosarcoma is a malignant vascular neoplasm that variably recapitulates the morphological and immunohistochemical features of endothelial cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65833,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "<p>9120/3 Angiosarcoma</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-15T12:31:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65834,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B56.Y &amp; XH6264 Angiosarcoma, other specified primary site &amp; Haemangiosarcoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65835,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> haemangiosarcoma; lymphangiosarcoma; malignant haemangioendothelioma; malignant angioendothelioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65836,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65837,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiosarcoma is more common in the uterus and ovary, but the vulva and vagina can be involved {24257655; 28595254; 19396453; 9591733}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65838,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients present with vaginal bleeding, anaemia, and/or nonspecific gastrointestinal symptoms {24257655}. Vulvar tumours may present as white papules {26704338}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65839,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The median patient age at presentation is 47&nbsp;years (range: 17&ndash;87&nbsp;years) {24257655}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65840,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most cases are sporadic. In a subset, radiation therapy and chronic lymphoedema are predisposing factors {28795130; 17581409; 16343203}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65841,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiosarcomas are genetically heterogeneous. Most harbour complex karyotypes, without recurrent chromosomal changes {20217954; 25231439}. High-level <em>MYC</em> gene amplifications (at 8q24) occur in almost all postradiation and chronic lymphoedema&ndash;associated secondary angiosarcomas, and rarely in primary angiosarcomas {20949568; 20008140; 24329959; 22121953}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65842,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "<p>Most tumours form a large, friable haemorrhagic mass with poorly defined margins and an ulcerated surface, but flat plaque-like tumours have been reported in the cervix {31815894}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-06T16:14:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65843,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most angiosarcomas have a lobulated architecture with closely packed, anastomosing abortive vascular channels; cystic spaces; and solid sheets of spindled and epithelioid cells with haemorrhage and necrosis. Tumour cells are pleomorphic and contain large, hyperchromatic nuclei. Mitoses are usually numerous. Prominent stromal hyalinization may be present. Immunohistochemically, the tumour cells stain for CD31, CD34, factor&nbsp;VIII, and ERG. Some angiosarcomas express D2-40 or cytokeratins. ER and PR are negative {24257655}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65844,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65845,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Demonstration of <em>MYC</em> amplification may be helpful, although only in the setting of radiation-associated or lymphoedema-associated angiosarcoma {22121953; 21909081; 20949568}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65846,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> architecturally complex vascular structures lined by atypical endothelial cells; solid patterns and epithelioid morphology may be seen.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> immunoreactivity for endothelial markers (in selected cases).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65847,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Staging of angiosarcoma is not recommended, because this neoplasm&rsquo;s typically aggressive natural history is not consistent with soft tissue staging systems.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65848,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 430,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>As is the case for soft tissue tumours in general, prognosis depends on stage (extent of disease). Higher-stage tumours are associated with a mean overall survival time of &lt;&nbsp;30&nbsp;months {24257655}. Much of the evidence is from uterine and cervical cases. Factors such as epithelioid change, necrosis, and margin status, which have been shown to be prognostically significant for angiosarcomas in general, require specific validation at gynaecological sites {20485141; 17974557}. Older patient age, retroperitoneal site, and large tumour size are associated with poor outcome {9630175; 17974557; 20485141}. The most frequent sites of distant metastasis are the lungs, followed by lymph nodes, soft tissues, bone, liver, and other sites (including the brain) {9630175; 15166670}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:26:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]